---
// What each v2 optimization is worth, measured against the alternative it
// replaced on an identical replay stream. Bars are the THROUGHPUT factor between
// the two (latency is power-of-two bucketed, so it's not plotted here).
// Allocation dominates: no-hot-path-allocation is the point of the whole chart.
const data = [
  { label: 'no hot-path allocation', factor: 5.3, kind: 'primary',
    note: 'pooled arena vs per-level heap · shape held at BTreeMap', dominant: true },
  { label: 'group-commit journal', factor: 63.5, kind: 'primary',
    note: 'batch-of-64 vs per-command fsync · on real disk' },
  { label: 'array-indexed ladder', factor: 1.12, kind: 'secondary',
    note: 'flat array vs BTreeMap · allocation held at arena' },
];
// log scale so 1.12× and 63.5× coexist legibly and the dominance still reads.
const minF = 1, maxF = 70;
const lx = (f) => (Math.log(f) - Math.log(minF)) / (Math.log(maxF) - Math.log(minF));
const W = 640, rowH = 52, top = 20, labelW = 186, barMax = 380;
const H = top + data.length * rowH + 30;
const x = (f) => Math.max(lx(f) * barMax, 3);
const fill = { primary: 'var(--accent)', secondary: 'var(--muted)' };
const ticks = [1, 2, 5, 10, 20, 50];
---
<figure class="chart">
  <svg viewBox={`0 0 ${W} ${H}`} role="img" aria-label="Throughput slowdown factor when each v2 design choice is regressed, one variable at a time. Removing the pooled arena (no hot-path allocation) costs 5.3 times throughput. Per-command fsync instead of group-commit costs 63.5 times. The flat array ladder versus a BTreeMap is only 1.12 times. Allocation and journal batching dominate; the array shape is a small secondary win.">
    <line x1={labelW} y1={top - 6} x2={labelW} y2={top + data.length * rowH} stroke="var(--border)" />
    {ticks.map((t) => {
      const tx = labelW + lx(t) * barMax;
      return (
        <g>
          <line x1={tx} y1={top - 6} x2={tx} y2={top + data.length * rowH} stroke="var(--border)" stroke-dasharray="2 4" opacity="0.5" />
          <text x={tx} y={top + data.length * rowH + 16} text-anchor="middle" class="ax">{t}×</text>
        </g>
      );
    })}
    {data.map((d, i) => {
      const y = top + i * rowH;
      const bw = x(d.factor);
      return (
        <g>
          <text x={labelW - 12} y={y + 15} text-anchor="end" class="lbl">{d.label}</text>
          {d.note.split(' · ').map((line, k) => (
            <text x={labelW - 12} y={y + 29 + k * 12} text-anchor="end" class="sub">{line}</text>
          ))}
          <rect x={labelW} y={y + 3} width={bw} height={rowH - 22} rx="2" fill={fill[d.kind]} />
          <text x={labelW + bw + 8} y={y + 18} class="val">×{d.factor}</text>
        </g>
      );
    })}
  </svg>
  <figcaption>Each optimization measured against the alternative it replaced, on the same 5M-command replay (every configuration returned a byte-identical 2,706,139 fills, so the difference is only speed). Bars are the <em>throughput</em> factor on a log axis. The array shape everyone diagrams is worth ×1.12. Not allocating while matching is worth ×5.3. The famous trick is the small lever.</figcaption>
</figure>

<style>
  .chart :global(.lbl) { font-size: 13px; fill: var(--fg); font-family: var(--font-sans); }
  .chart :global(.sub) { font-size: 10px; fill: var(--muted); font-family: var(--font-sans); }
  .chart :global(.val) { font-size: 13px; fill: var(--fg); font-family: var(--font-mono); }
  .chart :global(.ax)  { font-size: 11px; fill: var(--muted); font-family: var(--font-sans); }
</style>
